course: money
lesson: 7
title: Wants and needs
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A need is something you must have to live, stay healthy or stay safe, like food, water, a home, warm clothes and a coat in winter; a want is something nice that you could live without, like a trampoline or a new game.
explain: When Ava's coat has a big rip in winter and Mum has £40 spare, the £25 coat comes first and the £120 trampoline waits: needs come first, wants come after if there is money left.
explain: People often say "need" when they mean "want": nobody dies without the new FIFA, and calling it a want helps you decide calmly instead of in a panic.
explain: Some things are a need in a basic form and a want in a fancy form: shoes keep your feet dry, but the extra £60 for a famous label pays for the look and the name.
fact: A need is something you must have to live, stay healthy or stay safe.
fact: A want is something you would like but can live without.
fact: The basic needs are food, water, a home (shelter), clothes and safety.
fact: When money is limited, families pay for needs first and wants only if money is left.
fact: Food is a need, but sweets and treats are wants.
fact: Saying "I need it" about a game or toy is usually exaggerating; it is a want.
fact: Something can be both: plain shoes are a need, while expensive branded trainers are a want.
fact: A shop's own-brand product is often almost the same as the famous brand but cheaper, because you pay extra for the name and the adverts.
fact: Wanting things is normal and fine; the skill is deciding what comes first.
fact: A trade-off is giving up one thing to get another.
fact: Grown-ups face wants and needs too: a broken fridge that keeps food safe comes before a bigger telly.
exercise: Which of these is a need? | A warm coat in winter | A trampoline | A new video game | A phone case with glitter
exercise: Mila's old phone case works fine, but she wants a new £8 one. Is the new case a need or a want? | A want | A need | Both a need and a want | Neither
exercise: Bread £1.10, milk £1.20 and apples £1.70. What do these needs cost together? | £4.00 | £3.90 | £4.10 | £3.00
exercise: Sammy adds a £2 bag of sweets to the £4.00 of bread, milk and apples. What is the total? | £6.00 | £5.00 | £4.20 | £6.20
exercise: Mum has £40 spare. She buys Ava's £25 coat. How much is left? | £15 | £25 | £5 | £20
exercise: Why does the coat come before the £120 trampoline? | A warm coat in winter is a need, the trampoline a want | Coats are always cheaper than toys | Trampolines are not allowed in winter | Mum doesn't like trampolines
exercise: Rhys says he'll "literally die" without the £60 game. He gets £5 a week. How many weeks to save for it? | 12 | 10 | 6 | 15
exercise: Hana has £5. She needs a £3 pencil case. How much is left for her slime fund? | £2 | £3 | £5 | £1
exercise: How many of these are needs: water, home, food, toys, clothes, games? | 4 | 2 | 3 | 6
exercise: Branded cereal is £3.50 and own-brand is £1.50. How much is saved by buying own-brand? | £2.00 | £1.50 | £5.00 | £2.50
exercise: Why is own-brand cheaper? | You don't pay extra for the famous name and adverts | It is made from worse food that big brands refuse to use | It is past its use-by date, so the shop sells it off cheap | Shops lose money on it on purpose to get people in the door
exercise: Billy wants £90 trainers; the £30 ones keep his feet just as dry. How much extra is the cool pair? | £60 | £30 | £90 | £120
exercise: The fridge costs £300 and the family has £350. How much is left after buying it? | £50 | £150 | £650 | £100
exercise: Why does the fridge come before the new TV? | A fridge keeps food safe; the old TV still works | TVs are always more expensive, so the TV can wait longer | Fridges are on sale every week | The TV is a need and the fridge a want
exercise: What is a trade-off? | Giving up one thing to get another | Swapping cards in the playground | Getting two things for the price of one | A shop's special offer
exercise: Which of these is the basic list of needs? | Food, water, home, clothes and safety | Food, toys, games, a phone and a tablet | Water, sweets, a bike, a TV and pocket money | Home, holidays abroad, new trainers and pets
test: Sammy goes "sweets are a need, Mum, I'm literally starving." What's true? | Food is a need, but sweets are a want | Sweets are a need whenever you're feeling hungry | Sweets are healthier than bread | Starving means you need sweets
test: Rhys: "I NEED the new FIFA, I'll literally die." His sis: "you'll survive, ya drama queen." Who's right? | His sister: a game is a want, nobody dies without it | Rhys: games are a basic need | Both: games are needs on weekends | Neither: games are neither needs nor wants
test: Lottie goes "eww, it's not the real one" about own-brand flakes. Mum says "same flakes, love." What's the point? | Often you pay extra for the name, not better food | Own-brand is always worse food, made from cheaper bits | Famous brands are needs, own-brand is a want | Own-brand cereal is illegal in some shops
test: Billy: "the £30 ones aren't cool, man." His older cousin, a millennial, says "lowkey, nobody looks at your feet." ("Lowkey" means honestly, quietly.) What's the real choice? | Whether the look is worth £60 extra; both keep feet dry | Whether he wants dry feet at all | Whether £30 shoes are safe to wear | Whether he needs shoes at all
test: What would happen if a family spent all its money on wants first? | It might not have enough left for food, rent or warm clothes | It would get extra money from the shops | Needs would become free | Nothing, needs and wants cost the same
test: Hana has £5. Slime kit £5, pencil case for school £3. Her mate says "slime first, school stuff is boring". What's smarter? | Pencil case first, then save the £2 towards the slime | Slime first, the school will give her pencils | Buy both now with £5 | Buy neither and spend it on sweets
test: Ava's mum: "Coat first, then we'll talk trampoline." If she has £40 and the coat is £25, can she also buy the £120 trampoline? | No, only £15 would be left | Yes, with £15 to spare | Yes, exactly £40 is enough | No, she would be £25 short
test: A Texan aunt says "y'all fixin' to buy a new TV? Fridge first, sugar." ("Fixin' to" means about to.) Why? | The fridge is a need because it keeps food safe | TVs are against the rules in Texas during the summer | Fridges are always cheaper than televisions in shops | The TV is broken and the fridge still works fine
test: Mila says "I need a new case" but the old one isn't broken, "it's just ugly." What should she call it? | A want | A need | An emergency | A basic need
test: Theo's mate sorts "games" as a need "cos I'd be bored without them". Is he right? | No, being bored isn't the same as not staying alive or safe | Yes, anything you'd miss is a need | Yes, games are on the basic needs list | No, games are neither a want nor a need
test: Grandad says "back in my day a coat lasted ten winters." If a £25 coat lasts 5 winters, how much per winter? | £5 | £25 | £10 | £2.50
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a "need"? | An article or condition requisite to life, health or safety | Any article which a person earnestly desires | Any article bearing a well-known brand | Any article priced in excess of fifty pounds
exam: It is given that bread, milk and apples are priced at one pound ten pence, one pound twenty pence and one pound seventy pence respectively. Determine the aggregate cost. | Four pounds | Three pounds ninety pence | Four pounds ten pence | Three pounds
exam: Identify the course of action most consistent with prudence where funds suffice for only one of a refrigerator and a television, the existing television being serviceable. | The acquisition of the refrigerator | The acquisition of the television | The acquisition of neither | The borrowing of funds to acquire both forthwith
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning products sold under a retailer's own brand. | They are frequently of comparable quality at a lower price | They are invariably of inferior quality and unsafe | They are invariably dearer than branded products | They may not lawfully be sold as food
